    {
      "filename": "The Harari Cultural House (Gey Gar) ጌይ ጋር.jpg",
      "title": "File:The Harari Cultural House (Gey Gar) ጌይ ጋር.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/The_Harari_Cultural_House_%28Gey_Gar%29_%E1%8C%8C%E1%8B%AD_%E1%8C%8B%E1%88%AD.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Harari_Cultural_House_(Gey_Gar)_%E1%8C%8C%E1%8B%AD_%E1%8C%8B%E1%88%AD.jpg",
      "width": 4160,
      "height": 3120,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Naif Mohamed",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "source": "The Harari Cultural House (Gey Gar) ጌይ ጋር",
      "description": "Harari House\nNadabas most of the front part of the house floor is dominated by five flat raised stages of different size and level known as nadabas. These are gidir (large) nadaba, tit (small) nadaba, amir (king) nadaba, sutri (covered) nadaba and the gabitieher (back door) nadaba. Guests and family members are served by these nadabas based on their knowledge, age and societal status.",
      "dateCreated": "2022-10-19 10:46:41",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "harar-wk",
